<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amenemhat III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1831–1786 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century-bce">19th century BCE</a> · Also known as Amenemhet III</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amenemhat III was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the Twelfth Dynasty who reigned for about 45 years in the 19th century BCE, the height of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a>. He developed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a> with irrigation works, exploited the Sinai turquoise mines and built pyramids at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dahshur">Dahshur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawara">Hawara</a>, where his mortuary temple was later known to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labyrinth">Labyrinth</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii.jpg" alt="Amenemhat III" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: sailko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amenemhet_III,_basalto,_seconda_met%C3%A0_del_XIX_sec._ac._01_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amenemhat III, also written Amenemhet III, was the sixth king of the Twelfth Dynasty and the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii">Senusret III</a>. No text states the relationship outright, but it is inferred from their coregency. His mother is unknown. Two of his wives, Aat and Khenemetneferhedjet III, were buried in his pyramid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dahshur">Dahshur</a>, and his one confirmed daughter, Neferuptah, appears to have been groomed as his successor, since her name is written in a cartouche. She seems to have died before him.</p><p>The regnal dates roll over from Year 19 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii">Senusret III</a>, its highest dated attestation, to Year 1 of Amenemhat III, and the two are believed to have shared the throne for about twenty years, with Amenemhat III as the active king, though not all scholars accept a coregency.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The campaigns and domestic policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii">Senusret III</a> left Amenemhat III a stable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and there is little evidence of military expeditions in his reign: a small mission in Year 9 near the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumma">Kumma</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> is recorded in a rock inscription whose author says that nobody died on the return. A few expeditions went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a>.</p><p>Mining of turquoise and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> in the Sinai reached its peak, with more than fifty texts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serabit-el-khadim">Serabit el-Khadim</a>, Wadi Maghara and Wadi Nasb, and near-permanent settlements grew up round the workings. Quarries at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-hammamat">Wadi Hammamat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-el-hudi">Wadi el-Hudi</a>, Tura, Hatnub and Aswan were also worked.</p><p>These resources paid for a large building programme, above all in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a>, where he dedicated a temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobek">Sobek</a> and a chapel to Renenutet, set up two colossal statues of himself at Biahmu and contributed to the excavation of Lake Moeris. He built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bubastis">Bubastis</a> and enlarged the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serabit-el-khadim">Serabit el-Khadim</a> and of Ptah at Memphis. In his Year 30 he held his Sed festival, the jubilee that renews royal authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He built two pyramids, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dahshur">Dahshur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawara">Hawara</a>, the first king since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu</a> to build more than one, and was buried in the second, at Hawara, in his Year 46. Flinders Petrie found only burned fragments of bone and wood in the burial chamber, which suggests that robbers burned the coffins and mummy.</p><p>Late in his reign he made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iv">Amenemhat IV</a> a junior coregent, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobekneferu">Sobekneferu</a> later succeeded Amenemhat IV as the last ruler of the dynasty. Scholars link the lack of a recorded male heir to the decline of the Twelfth Dynasty. Amenemhat III was deified and worshipped in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a> for centuries. His statues of his later years show heavy eyelids, sunken cheeks and deep lines, a departure from the idealised youth of earlier kings, according to the art historian Gay Robins.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amenemhat III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Middle Bronze Age local rulers such as Abishemu and his son Ipshemuabi adopted Egyptian forms, writing their names in hieroglyphs with the Egyptian title of a provincial governor and receiving gifts from the pharaohs <b>Amenemhat III</b> and Amenemhat IV, objects found in their rock-cut tombs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Rhind Mathematical Papyrus, one of the main sources for Egyptian mathematics, was copied under the Hyksos from an original written in the reign of <b>Amenemhat III</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
